1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,692
Today I'm going to talk about the eucalyptus tree,

2
00:00:05,692 --> 00:00:10,520
this is a very common tree here in Australia

3
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:14,490
where it's also sometimes called the gum tree.

4
00:00:14,490 --> 00:00:18,670
First, I'm going to talk about why it's important,

5
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:23,780
then I'm going to describe some problems it faces at present.

6
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:30,000
Right. Well, the eucalyptus tree is an important tree for lots of reasons.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,370
For example, it gives shelter to creatures like birds and bats,

8
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:39,840
and these and other species also depend on it for food

9
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:43,670
particularly the nectar from its flowers.

10
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:47,280
So, it supports biodiversity.

11
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:50,551
It's useful to us humans too,

12
00:00:50,551 --> 00:00:58,730
because we can kill germs with a disinfectant made from oil extracted from eucalyptus leaves.

13
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:06,550
The eucalyptus grows all over Australia and the trees can live for up to 400 years.

14
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:13,212
So, it's alarming that all across the country numbers of eucalyptus are falling

15
00:01:13,212 --> 00:01:16,800
because the trees are dying off prematurely.

16
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,220
So, what are the reasons for this?

17
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:23,820
One possible reason is disease.

18
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:26,840
As far back as the 1970s

19
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:31,270
the trees started getting a disease called Mundulla Yellows,

20
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:37,230
the trees leaves would gradually turn yellow, then the tree would die.

21
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:44,957
It wasn't until 2004 that they found the cause of the problem was lime or calcium hydroxide,

22
00:01:44,957 --> 00:01:47,940
to give it its proper chemical name

23
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:52,230
which was being used in the construction of roads.

24
00:01:52,230 --> 00:01:55,720
The lime was being washed away into the ground

25
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:59,980
and affecting the roots of the eucalyptus trees nearby.

26
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:07,910
What it was doing was preventing the trees from sucking up the iron they needed for healthy growth.

27
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:14,370
When this was injected back into the affected trees, they immediately recovered.

28
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:20,000
But this problem only affected a relatively small number of trees.

29
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:31,950
By 2000, huge numbers of eucalyptus were dying along Australia's east coast of a disease known as "Bell-miner Associated Die-back".

30
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:34,440
The bell-miner is a bird

31
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:40,290
and the disease seems to be common where there are high populations of bell-miners.

32
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:44,560
Again, it's the leaves of the trees that are affected.

33
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:51,220
What happens is that insects settle on the leaves and eat their way round them

34
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:53,200
destroying them as they go.

35
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:59,240
And at the same time, they secrete a solution which has sugar in it.

36
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:03,060
The bell-miner birds really like this solution

37
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:05,699
and in order to get as much as possible,

38
00:03:05,699 --> 00:03:10,140
they keep away other creatures that might try to get it.

39
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:16,362
So these birds and insects flourish at the expense of other species,

40
00:03:16,362 --> 00:03:23,620
and eventually so much damage is done to the leaves that the tree dies.

41
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:34,170
But experts say that trees can start looking sick before any sign of "Bell-miner Associated Die-back".

42
00:03:34,170 --> 00:03:39,440
So it looks as if the problem might have another explanation.

43
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:45,940
One possibility is that it's to do with the huge bushfires that we have in Australia.

44
00:03:45,940 --> 00:04:00,250
A theory proposed over 40 years ago by ecologist William Jackson is that the frequency of bushfires in a particular region affects the type of vegetation that grows there.

45
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:04,260
If there are very frequent bushfires in a region

46
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:08,340
this encourages grass to grow afterwards.

47
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:11,880
While, if the bushfires are rather less frequent

48
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:16,020
this results in the growth of eucalyptus forests.

49
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:18,470
So why is this?

50
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:25,480
Why do fairly frequent bushfires actually support the growth of eucalyptus.

51
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:32,296
Well, one reason is that the fire stops the growth of other species

52
00:04:32,296 --> 00:04:36,490
which would consume water needed by eucalyptus trees.

53
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:42,422
And there's another reason, if these other quick growing species of bushes

54
00:04:42,422 --> 00:04:45,388
and plants are allowed to proliferate,

55
00:04:45,388 --> 00:04:52,308
they harm the eucalyptus in another way by affecting the composition of the soil

56
00:04:52,308 --> 00:04:54,780
and removing nutrients from it.

57
00:04:54,780 --> 00:05:03,850
So some bushfires are actually essential for the eucalyptus to survive as long as they are not too frequent.

58
00:05:03,850 --> 00:05:15,470
In fact, there's evidence that Australia's indigenous people practised regular burning of bush land for thousands of years before the arrival of the Europeans.

59
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:18,860
But since Europeans arrived on the continent

60
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:22,810
the number of bushfires has been strictly controlled.

61
00:05:22,810 --> 00:05:33,286
Now scientists believe that this reduced frequency of bushfires to low levels has led to what is known as dry rainforest,

62
00:05:33,286 --> 00:05:40,270
which seems an odd name as usually we associate tropical rainforest with wet conditions.

63
00:05:40,270 --> 00:05:44,550
And what special about this type of rainforest?

64
00:05:44,550 --> 00:05:49,450
Well, unlike tropical rainforest, which is a rich ecosystem

65
00:05:50,050 --> 00:05:54,140
this type of ecosystem is usually a simple one.

66
00:05:54,140 --> 00:06:00,650
It has very thick, dense vegetation, but not much variety of species.

67
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:03,690
The vegetation provides lots of shade

68
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:09,165
so one species that does find it ideal is the bell-miner bird,

69
00:06:09,165 --> 00:06:12,950
which builds its nests in the undergrowth there.

70
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:17,410
But again, that's not helpful for the eucalyptus tree.
